{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 claims the §11.3 flag 'persists because of a type confusion in the critic harness' — but this frames the problem as a defect in the *harness* (an auditing mechanism external to the candidate), whereas the problem context says the ritual 'flags sustained' (§11.3), which is about whether a defect in the *critic target under audit* persists. The artifact asserts the mechanism that performed adjudication is confused, but never identifies what the candidate-as-critic did wrong to cause the sustained flag in the first place; thus it misattributes the fault to the harness's adjudication rather than demonstrating a genuine flaw in the audited critic's own reasoning. Without showing an actual error in the target critic's object- or meta-level judgments, the 'type confusion' assertion is an unsupported diagnosis rather than an auditable fault.","premise":"The problem asks to audit the critic such that 'adjudication-ritual flags sustained (§11.3)' is the concern — i.e., to show whether the target critic's defect persists. SRC_001 instead asserts the *harness* conflates meta-level and object-level judgments, blaming the auditor rather than identifying a flaw in the audited critic.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"7374616e646172642074686520746172676574","quote":"audit the critic: adjudication-ritual flags sustained (§11.3)"},{"block":"737263303031636c61696d","quote":"The §11.3 flag persists because of a type confusion in the critic harness: it conflates a meta-level judgment ... with an object-level judgment ..."}],"counterexample":null}]}